-- chem/sylvester.cub — Sylvester's law of inertia, proved (it closes the first open item of chem/collapse.cub). Over a
-- type A with its operations, every law a hypothesis where it is used, in three bundles: sy-DL (a commutative ring
-- without zero divisors whose zero is decided by isz), sy-QL (a sign: x ≤ 0 or x ≥ 0 with its strict form; closed under
-- sums; c²·d has the sign of d; a zero sum of two of one sign has both zero; c²·d = 0 with d strict gives c = 0), and
-- sy-BL (a symmetric form, additive and homogeneous in its first argument). The proof, in the order of the module.
-- (1) The quadratic form on a combination: on a family orthogonal under F, F(Σ c_i u_i, Σ c_i u_i) = Σ c_i² F(u_i, u_i)
-- (sy-quad). (2) The core (sy-core): a family orthogonal under F where F is negative and one where F is positive admit
-- no dependence Σ c_i u_i + Σ b_j p_j = 0 with a nonzero coefficient: with v = Σ c_i u_i and w = Σ b_j p_j, v + w = 0
-- gives F(v, v) = F(w, w), one a sum of nonpositives and the other of nonnegatives, so both are zero and every
-- coefficient is. (3) The law from a bound on the dimension (sy-law): where every family of more than n vectors has a
-- dependence with a nonzero coefficient (sy-Dim), a negative family and a positive one have at most n members together
-- (sy-bound), so two witnesses of n vectors each, split into a negative and a positive orthogonal family, have the same
-- number of negative vectors. (4) The dimension of A^n (sy-vn-dim): more than n vectors of A^n are dependent, by
-- fraction-free elimination on the first coordinate, one coordinate at a time (sy-dim-step: W of dimension at most n
-- gives A × W of dimension at most n + 1; the pivot is the first vector whose first coordinate is not zero, those
-- before it are kept apart, and the rest are reduced against it by (b, y) ↦ (−b)·x + a·y). (5) sy-inertia: the law on
-- A^n for every symmetric bilinear form, with no hypothesis of dimension. Not proved here: that a matrix form on tuples
-- satisfies sy-BL and that chem/collapse.cub's count on a Gram matrix (cl-count) is the number of negative vectors of
-- such a split witness; the sign laws sy-QL from the axioms of an ordered ring; and the laws for the binary rationals
-- in lowest terms (they need the correctness of bin-gcd, which the store does not hold).
import "collapse.cub"

